WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:03.660
I guess it's my turn to choose a card.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Let's see.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.080
What's really going on in "Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared"?

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.940
Yes, if only there was some way to learn more information about this.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:23.520
I'm a computer, I'm a computery guy, I got some theories in line that just might blow your mind

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:32.500
*Wicked beats ensue*

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.500
*Painfully high pitched squeak*

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.640
Hello, internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.240
When we watch our internet videos a quarter frame at a time!

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.500
You think I'm joking but when it comes to 'Don't Hug Me I'm Scared,' it's pretty accurate.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:01:01.100
These videos are so densely packed with detail and symbolism that you can watch them over, and over, and over, again and still come up with new easter eggs

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.560
you've never seen before!

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.600
Just look at this one scene at the end of the 3rd episode of 'Don't Hug Me.'

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:13.540
The first time you watch, you might see Roy, Yellow Guy's dad, standing there while his son is indoctrinated into the cult of Malcom.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:19.900
Keep looking and you'll notice The Notepad and Clock from the prior 2 episodes standing there behind him dressed in white robes.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.280
Keep searching and you see Red Guy and Duck there too, but they're supposed to be at their delicious chicken picnic,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.640
unaware of where Yellow Guy even is.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.240
Or how about here in episode 5, when Duck quits the show?

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.900
There are a few frames of Red Guy's spaghetti head in the microwave and a few frames later,

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.460
His legs where The Meat Man was standing just seconds before.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.260
And it isn't just limited to the videos, at the end of episode 5, we see Red guy leaving a phone booth.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.680
Google search for the phone number on that phone booth and you find this hidden video.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:56.260
*The Adventures of Red Guy!*

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.240
So are those duck boats the smoking gun that decode everything that's going on in this series?

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.860
*Drum roll!*

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:02.760
No,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:03.680
No they aren't.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.820
But you can bet I took it all into account in my creation in todays theory.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.740
Because last time we covered the easy stuff:

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:15.580
The symbolism of the story and how it represents a children's television show, corrupted by the influence of advertising.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.840
But today, instead of looking up what 'Don't Hug Me, I'm Scared' means in our world,

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.700
we're going to explain what it means in its own world.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:29.500
Who are these characters? What is the deal with all these easter eggs? And how should we be interperting the ending?

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.280
Is it even an ending at all?!

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.320
I've got the answers all here for you, and by the end of this video,

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.620
I guarantee to have you, digital dancing!

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:43.140
Now as I watched and re-watched the series, even though Red Guy, Yellow Guy and Duck are the ones who appear on camera most often,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.740
these videos seem to have 2 main characters:

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.040
Red Guy, yes, and Roy,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.820
Yellow Guy's porn surfing father.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.980
And that's reinforced in episode 6, where the whole ending sequence of events

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.440
revolves around the relationship between these 2.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:58.100
But who are they?

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:03.200
Additionally in a series full of bizarre moments, 2 events seem to be the most game changing.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.400
Red Guy's literal mind blow at the end of The Computer episode,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.740
and the dramatic shift to real life in the middle of episode 6.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.920
It was my theory that if we could some how answer who those 2 characters are,

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.920
and what those 2 events stood for,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.200
then this whole enigma ball would unravel.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.780
So I started with the guy that we have the most information about:

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:21.700
Red Guy.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.040
Now as we established last time, 'Don't Hug Me' depicts a television show,

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.600
With Red Guy, Yellow Guy and Duck as performers on the series.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.040
Red Guy: "Oh there you are, we've been looking for you all afternoon."

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.000
Don't get me wrong, I didn't say that they're good performers,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.820
but they are performers none the less.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.020
In addition to all the camera and set imagery we discussed last time,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.320
there are multiple instances where things don't go as planned,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:50.180
and Red Guy literally breaks the 4th wall to address what seems like someone behind the camera.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.060
The first instance of this occurs in episode 2, when the clock begins to talk.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.780
Red Guy turns to the camera, genuinely confused.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.920
Saying quietly, "What? Who is that?"

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.920
Than again at the beginning of episode 4, The Red Guy delivers his setup

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.320
Red Guy: "If only there was a way to learn more about the world."

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.320
Then strongly indicates to the globe.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:11.040
Even the music queue follows in.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.920
Duck then joins in, trying to queue the reveal of the globe,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.580
but both are genuinely caught off guard by the computer coming to life,

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.120
apparently a change in the script that neither were made aware of.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.020
It's made especially clear that Red Guy wasn't in on this change when he reveals that they already had another computer,

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.460
a cute laptop,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.480
but is once again cut off before he can get through is thought.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:35.200
From there Red Guy tries to shut the computer up by saying things like:

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.080
"Wait a second" "Stop talking" "Be quiet"

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.480
Before touching the computer and causing it to freak out.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:47.500
So clearly Red Guy is both unaware of and very displeased by these unexpected changes.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.600
So then who is he?

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.500
Well that answer comes to us in the final episode "Dreams."

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.260
It starts with Yellow Guy left alone learning about dreams from The Lamp,

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:02.600
but quickly transitions to Red Guy IRL at a desk job and singing the creativity song at a karaoke bar

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:06.040
to receive, let's just say, less than stellar reviews.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.360
Now most other theories see this as occurring in the present day,

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:16.400
but what makes more sense, is that scene is actually a flash back to The Red Guy's life before the show,

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.500
when he was working at a boring desk job and had dreams of making a children's television program.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:25.140
I mean look at the way he pitches the idea of the singing file to his boss.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.480
Something that become physically manifested later in the video,

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:29.480
more on that in a minute.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:33.400
But truly despite the monotone voice this guy is a guy with dreams!

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.880
Dreams that sometimes seem like they're getting drowned in a pool of oil,

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.780
or booed off stage with all the enthusiasm of a YouTube comment section.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:45.540
But within that sea of bored disapproval, one person watching The Red Guy's performance

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.080
wasn't booing: Roy.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.680
And it's here that we finally learn of the secret to their relationship.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.700
Roy discovered Red Guy at an open mic night and made him a deal to sponsor the kids show

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:57.760
that Red guy had always wanted.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.160
But then how do we know that Roy is the sponsor?

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:03.300
It's easy, we're expressly told it in the credits.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.680
Episodes 2 through 6 of DHMIS and with a special thank you

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.480
to all the kick starter backers, and the last person thanked in every episode is Roy.

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.580
Because he too is a financial backer.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.400
In fact, within the lore of the series, he's the main backer.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.840
A sponsor with greedy motives who gets out of hand

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.160
once Red Guy is out of the picture in episode 5.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:26.220
We see very clearly in the healthy eating episode that Roy sells food products.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.720
His name is littered everywhere!

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.900
But more specifically notice the food his name is on:

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:36.340
Roy's flour, Roy's health juice, Roy's oats, and Roy's flakes.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.000
Most of these are grain based.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.840
Now compare that to what The Meat and The Can are teaching the children as healthy:

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.420
All foods that are grain or dairy based.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.000
And also, Aspic.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.640
But this theme of questionable health benefits behind plain foods

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.960
doesn't stop there.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.820
It also appears in a bizarre interview the characters did with the British Website 'It's Nice That.'

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.960
In May of 2016, in it, Red Guy says

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.960
that he's trying an all plain foods diet

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:03.800
and that it's causing some disturbing side effects.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:05.220
Wonder if his teeth are turning gray.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.760
In fact, Roy's role as a food producer can even tie in with the overalls that he and his son wear.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:13.340
This isn't the first time that we've seen some product placement in these episodes.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:16.620
In episode 4, the computer prints a picture of oats cereal,

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:20.180
and then also shows them a picture of the same cereal in the newspaper.

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:23.680
If you look really closely, you can even see the oats digital dancing.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.380
Considering the theory that Red Guy is the creator, and Roy is the sponsor,

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:30.020
this product placement was probably a big point in contention.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.860
But with Red Guy no longer on the show as of episode 5,

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:37.560
Roy was free to fill the video with as many products and skewed health messages as he wanted to,

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:41.440
turning it from being educational, to being one big commercial.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.180
You can even see it happen.

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.900
If you check out the final moments of episode 4, frame by frame,

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.660
You see both Duck and Yellow Guy glitch into the picture.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.460
But look closely and you'll see their brains are exposed,

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:53.680
a sign that they've been brainwashed by the computer.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.380
So if Roy's the sponsor, than what's the deal with Yellow Guy calling him his dad?

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.140
Well, he's not lying.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:02.360
As sponsor of the show, Roy can make sure that his son is put into the spotlight.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:07.300
This would explain why Yellow Guy is clearly the most inexperienced of the three on camera.

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:11.060
But don't think for a second that Roy is some stage mom trying to get his son famous.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:12.160
Quite the contrary!

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:17.620
Going back to that "It's Nice That" interview, we actually hear Roy speak, for the only time ever

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:18.940
throughout the entire series.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.940
When asked with if he had anything to say, he responds with the following:

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:26.060
"My silly boy has allowed his eyes to grow arrogant and rude,

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:29.120
for this I will take him on a trip to punish land."

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:30.500
Yikes, punish land!

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:34.180
Something tells me this isn't Disney World's latest park extension!

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:36.640
But when you look at the series, it actually makes a lot of sense:

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.860
Yellow Guy has been the victim from the very beginning!

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:44.560
In episode 1, his painting is destroyed, and he's told his favourite colour isn't creative.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.180
In episode 2, the clock yells at him until his ears bleed,

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.740
In episode 3, he's nearly seduced into joining a cult,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.020
In episode 4, he gets brain washed by the computer,

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.800
In episode 5, he pulls a Scott Tenorman and eats his friend,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:00.940
And then in episode 6, he's tortured by an un-ending barrage of inane lessons,

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:02.680
Oh yeah, and also drowns in oil.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:07.580
But if you think Roy's punish land stops there, well boy howdy you are wrong!

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:11.940
Observant viewers will spot horses sprinkled all over DHMIS videos:

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:16.240
Pictures of horses, rocking horses, horse figurines, animated horses.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.740
Well, in the same interview with "It's Nice That" it's revealed that Yellow Guy is scared of horses.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:26.260
He's also apparently allergic to eggs, wasps, crab, meat and trees,

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:30.700
all of which are elements that we see worked into various episodes throughout the series.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:33.120
Roy's not only sponsoring the series to push his own goods,

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.280
he's also doing it to push his son to the break of insanity.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:42.900
Even 'Don't Hug Me I'm Scared's most famous easter egg fits in with the idea of Roy teaching Yellow Guy a lesson

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:48.100
The un-changing date of June 19th has bothered would-be theorists since the second episode.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.380
But we have our answer right now:

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.000
June 19th 1955, the date which we see hinted at over, and over again throughout series,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.680
was Father's Day.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:05.620
In 2011, when the first episode of DHMIS was released, Father's Day was also on June 19th.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:09.680
And this year, the date of release for the very last episode, was June 19th,

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.700
which low and behold, was once again, Father's Day.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:17.880
Roy is punishing his son for "letting his eyes grow arrogant and rude,"

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.880
maybe something about catching his dad watching porn.

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:25.820
But regardless, he forces him to respect his authority, by making every day Father's Day.

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:28.920
A constant reminder of who is in charge.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:33.860
As long as the date is June 19th, Yellow Guy is doomed to suffer this torture,

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:35.080
with no end in sight.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.320
Or at least that's what would've happened, if not for Red Guy.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.180
Which means it's finally time talk about the ending!

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:45.200
At the end of episode 4, Red Guy resists being lured into Roy's computer world,

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:48.100
leaving the living room set with Roy hidden in the corner,

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:51.480
and instead noticing that the computer is hooked up to a mysterious red cable

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:52.920
leading to the next room.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:56.500
When he opens the door, it's revealed the he's covered in motion tracking balls,

00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:00.420
and discovers a super low tech version of episode 1 being recorded.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.260
Then his head explodes.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:02.640
So what does all this mean?

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.420
Well the head explosion is definitely a literal version of Red Guy's mind being blown,

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:09.380
but what exactly blew his mind in the first place?

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.320
Many have sad it's him realizing he's on a T.V show,

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:16.580
but as we've seen already, both he and Duck break the 4th wall repeatedly,

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:18.280
meaning they know they're on camera.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:21.000
Instead, I think the revelation is a lot more positive.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.580
Notice that he follows a red cable hooked up to the computer,

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:25.780
and that he's leaving Roy's room.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:28.300
A room mind you, with higher production value

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:30.340
filled with props, and motion capture ability,

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:33.700
only to find himself in a room with a crude set and props.

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:38.780
Red Guy's mind blow in this moment, is that he doesn't need the dominating presence of Roy,

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.040
that he can create his series on a low budget,

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:44.680
and load it directly onto the computer, I.E a place like YouTube,

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:46.380
without needing a bunch of money.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:49.140
Remember, episode 4 is all about what you can do on a computer.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:52.160
According to Roy, it's all superficial consumerism things:

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:54.960
Charts, digital style, dancing.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:58.820
But here, Red Guy sees a video being filmed and uploaded directly to a computer.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.860
And again, based on what we discussed last video,

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:05.800
This is similar to the journey the creators of DHMIS took to keep their series independent

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:10.800
So, Red guy leaves Roy's series, than in episode 5, repeatedly see imagery of him,

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:12.720
on the outside, looking in.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.200
He's looking at the series from a new outsiders perspective.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.560
Those foreboding phone calls for Duck throughout the episode,

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:19.900
Are coming from him.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:23.280
We see him exiting a phone booth at the end of the episode for crying out loud.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:26.800
It's him calling his friends to let them know there's another option.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:32.400
That they can and need to escape, to stop selling their souls to Roy.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.960
Duck answers, and tries to get away, but as we see, he is captured, canned, and consumed.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:41.000
But is Duck getting eaten literal? It's hard to say,

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:42.940
It seems like it shouldn't be, right?

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:49.420
But than again, Roy is a farmer, and Duck is the only character who happens to be an animal.

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:50.400
Huh...

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:54.220
The can consuming the duck could be the duck getting canned.

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:54.960
Huh...

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.800
All of this come to a head in episode 6,

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:01.820
Yellow guy is the last one standing on the show, held there by his father.

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:04.840
Once the episode gets under way, we see the origins of the series.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.700
With Red guy being discovered by Roy at karaoke.

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:12.080
We then cut to Red guy discovering, for lack of a better term, the 'Show Machine',

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:16.600
Which creates the characters in situations that we've seen throughout the previous 5 episodes.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:18.840
Now mind you, none of this is literal.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:20.120
It's all meant to be symbolic.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:23.160
This is Red guy coming into grips with the show he created,

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:28.540
And seeing for the first time ever with clear eyes, the affect it's having on kids like Yellow guy.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:31.080
Roy discovers Red guy trying to shut down the machine,

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:34.120
and reaches his hand out to touch the Red Guy on the shoulder

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:37.160
It's not a gesture of aggression, more like a peace offering.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:40.560
An invitation to come back and work for the show once again.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.920
Let's face it, what else does Red guy have?

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:47.720
In a society full of negativity towards his ideas, Roy was the only one willing to take a chance on it.

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:50.820
And you seem that's the brilliance of episode 6 here.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.240
It starts by addressing typical dreams,

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.860
The ones that happen at night, but when you look again,

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.260
It's actually about personal dreams.

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:02.740
And how seeing a dream achieved, like Red guy finally creating the show he always wanted,

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:04.840
can turn into a waking nightmare.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:12.400
But it's at that moment Red guy decides to literally pull the plug on the show, and both his relationship with Roy,

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:14.580
eager to see what will happen next.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:18.780
And it works, we restart the series, but with some very important differences.

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:22.680
All the characters are now the colour they said was their favorite in episode 1,

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:24.940
Blue, Red and Green

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:28.920
The date on the calendar changes from June 19th, to June 20th.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.660
Meaning, that it's no longer Father's day

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.880
and Roy has no influence on the show anymore.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:36.120
It is quite literally the start of a new day.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:40.540
And that also makes sense when you look at the sets. At the beginning of episode 1, the kitchen is full of props,

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:43.400
Like knifes, and plates and books and a telephone,

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:46.980
But the reset version at the end of episode 6, is very simple,

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:48.520
With just a table and a few other items,

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.940
Without Roy's sponsorship, they're working on a smaller budget,

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:54.740
But the characters are finally free.

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:57.800
The notepad flips open and the first episode begins again,

00:14:57.800 --> 00:15:00.440
But this time, it's on their own terms.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:03.900
This time green will be, a creative colour...

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:09.100
But hey, that's just a theory, a Film Theory!

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:11.120
Aaaaaaaaaand, cut!

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.420
At least that's my interpretation.

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:26.060
And at the heart of it DHMIS is all about creativity, and interpretation.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:29.320
I've seen theories that are way different from this one that I've laid out for you.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.720
But they all stil deserve your respect and attention.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:34.220
After all, when asked by the UK news site 'Metro',

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:38.300
about fan theories for DHMIS, co-creator Becky Slone said:

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:43.180
'We have read a lot of theories online about what it means and they are all correct.'

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:46.540
So go watch the show again, go look for your own easter eggs,

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.800
Come up with your own theory and leave it in the comments below.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.660
There's only one rule though guys, be creative.

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:54.200
And with that we leave behind, DHMIS.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:59.980
As we close the book on this two parter, I gotta ask, over the six episodes of DHM, which was your favorite one?

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:05.460
Creativity, Time, Love, Computers, Healthy Eating or Dreams?

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:09.240
Click on one to choose, I'm really curious to see which one you responded to the most.

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:11.380
I'm a toss up between creativity and computers.

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:12.860
The love one was good too,

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:16.680
I'm just glad to be done with this, I have been watching these videos over and over and over again,

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:19.660
And most of the time it's at like midnight, or 1 in the morning.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:22.400
These are not things you wanna watch a bunch of times before you go to bed.

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.500
TRUST ME, no really, TRUST ME.

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.440
Any way, click on one to choose and cast your vote!

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:28.860
Can't wait to see what the results are.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:31.660
Now if you excuse me I'm going through some super hero withdrawals,

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:35.360
or, should I say, Super Villain withdrawals?

